n the hinterland of the Gold Coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ansar-sudan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ansar-sudan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ansar (Sudan)</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ansar ('helpers') were the followers of Muhammad Ahmad, the Mahdi of Sudan, who rose against Turco-Egyptian rule in 1881.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/anscar-i-of-ivrea"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/anscar-i-of-ivrea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Anscar I of Ivrea</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Anscar I was a Burgundian noble who supported Guy of Spoleto's bid for the crowns of France and Italy in 888.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/anscarids"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/anscarids.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Anscarids</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Anscarids were a dynasty descended from Anscar I, margrave of Ivrea in north-western Italy in the late 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/anschluss"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/anschluss.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Anschluss</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Anschluss was the annexation of Austria by Nazi Germany in March 1938.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ansedonia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ansedonia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ansedonia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ansedonia is a hilltop locality on the coast of southern Tuscany, part of the comune of Orbetello.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/anselm-erb"><span class="enc-name">Anselm Erb</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Anselm Erb was abbot of the Benedictine Imperial Abbey of Ottobeuren in Swabia from 1740 until his death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/anselm-of-canterbury"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/anselm-of-canterbury.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Anselm of Canterbury</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Anselm of Canterbury (c. 1033–1109) was an Italian-born monk, abbot of Bec in Normandy and from 1093 Archbishop of Canterbury.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/anselm-of-havelberg"><span class="enc-name">Anselm of Havelberg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Anselm of Havelberg was a German churchman and theologian, bishop of Havelberg from 1129 and archbishop of Ravenna from 1155.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/anseung"><span class="enc-name">Anseung</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Anseung was a member of the Goguryeo royal family whom Silla recognised as king of Goguryeo in 670, after the fall of Goguryeo to Silla and Tang China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ansfried-of-utrecht"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ansfried-of-utrecht.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ansfried of Utrecht</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ansfried was a count in the Low Countries who served Emperor Otto III before becoming bishop of Utrecht in 995.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ansgar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ansgar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ansgar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ansgar was a Frankish monk and missionary, known as the Apostle of the North.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/anshan"><span class="enc-name">Anshan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Anshan was an ancient city in the highlands of south-western Iran, at the site of Tal-e Malyan in Fars.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/antaeus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/antaeus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Antaeus</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Antaeus was a giant of Greek mythology, a son of Poseidon and Gaia, who lived in Libya and forced travellers to wrestle with him, killing them.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/antalaotra"><span class="enc-name">Antalaotra</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Antalaotra were Muslim, Swahili-speaking merchant communities of north-western Madagascar, of mixed East African, Arab and Malagasy descent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/antalas"><span class="enc-name">Antalas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Antalas was a Berber chief who ruled a principality around Capsa (Gafsa) in southern Byzacena, in present-day Tunisia, in the 6th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/antalo"><span class="enc-name">Antalo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Antalo is a village in the Tigray region of northern Ethiopia, south of Mekelle near Hintalo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/antalya"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/antalya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Antalya</span><span class="enc-type">Place</spa